## Issues

- [x] **`src/tools/upgrade/.aide` line 90 AND `src/index.ts` line 148 — apply-mode manifest status says `"matches"` but handler emits `"unchanged"`**
  The new desired outcome (registered-description outcome, lines 86-104 of the spec)
  stated that the second call's manifest carries `status` of
  `"updated"`, `"created"`, or `"matches"`. The registered description in `src/index.ts`
  repeated this: `status` (`"updated"`, `"created"`, or `"matches"`). Both were wrong.
  The actual handler returns `"unchanged"` for files that already matched canonical —
  `applyFiles/index.ts` line 54-56 maps `status === "matches"` → `"unchanged"`, and the
  `UpgradeFileStatus` type includes `"unchanged"` as a distinct terminal value.
  The correct three-value apply-mode vocabulary is `"updated"` / `"created"` /
  `"unchanged"`, which the *pre-existing* desired outcome (lines 46-49) already stated
  correctly. This cycle's new desired outcome and description both copied the wrong term.
  **Traces to:** desired outcome (lines 86-104) — lockstep invariant. Also trips
  undesired outcome (lines 151-169) — wrong status term in description.
  **Misalignment:** `spec-gap`
  **Fix landed:** `"matches"` changed to `"unchanged"` in the spec's desired outcome
  (line 90) and in the registered description in `src/index.ts` (line 148). Handler
  (`applyFiles/index.ts`) was already correct and was not touched. All 99 upgrade tests
  pass. No new drift found on re-verification.

## Verified Clean (no issues)

The following newly-added outcomes were verified and found fully satisfied:

**Desired — Brain detection-only contract (lines 71-83):**
- `parseBrainAide` is called with `projectRoot`; result maps `ok→matches`, `missing→missing`,
  both malformed kinds→`malformed`. No `differs` state is reachable. Verified in
  `upgrade/index.ts` lines 178-191 and confirmed by tests 11-15 in `index.test.ts`.
- `applyFiles` brain branch (lines 49-51) returns `instructions` for missing/malformed and
  never calls `mkdir` or `writeFile`. Verified in `applyFiles/index.ts` and confirmed by
  3 unit tests in `applyFiles/index.test.ts`.

**Desired — Ten-category vocabulary and brain in description (lines 86-104, excluding the `"matches"` error above):**
- The registered `category` Zod enum (index.ts line 164) lists exactly ten values.
- The description's category list names all ten in canonical order.
- Brain instructions clause is present and accurate.

**Undesired — No scaffolding inside upgrade (lines 133-138):**
- No `parseBrainAide` template, no brain.aide write path, no brain-root interview anywhere
  in `upgrade/index.ts` or `applyFiles/index.ts`. Clean.

**Undesired — No byte comparison for brain (lines 139-144):**
- Brain block uses `parseBrainAide` result, not `compareFile`. The `differs` status is
  structurally impossible for the brain category. Clean.

**Undesired — No silent omission (lines 145-150):**
- Brain is the tenth entry in the `categories` assembly (index.ts line 204); always
  present regardless of `parseBrainAide` result. Test 6 asserts `categories.toContain("brain")`.
  Clean.

**Undesired — No description-vs-handler drift on brain-specific claims (lines 151-169):**
- Description correctly says brain never writes and routes to `/aide:brain config`. No
  claim of `differs` status for brain. No eleventh category enumerated. Clean — the only
  drift is the `"matches"`/`"unchanged"` vocabulary error already reported above.

## Retro

The `"matches"` vocabulary error would have been caught at **spec authoring time** if the
author had cross-referenced the new desired outcome's status list against the pre-existing
desired outcome on lines 46-49 before committing the spec amendment. Both outcomes describe
the same apply-mode manifest; they must use the same terminal vocabulary.

A lightweight spec self-consistency check — specifically, comparing all status-vocabulary
enumerations within the same spec file against each other and against `UpgradeFileStatus`
in `src/types/index.ts` — would catch this class of error. The prior QA cycle (previous
`todo.aide`) fixed exactly this mismatch in the opposite direction (code said `"matches"`,
spec said `"unchanged"`); this cycle's amendment reintroduced the same drift in the spec
and description while leaving the code correct.

**Stage to strengthen:** spec amendment review — when adding a new desired outcome that
describes the same runtime contract as a pre-existing one, explicitly compare vocabulary
against all prior outcomes touching the same return shape.

**Cycle close (2026-04-27):** Fix landed cleanly. Both mutation points (`src/tools/upgrade/.aide`
line 90 and `src/index.ts` line 148) now read `"unchanged"` in place of `"matches"`. The
handler was not touched — it was already the reference implementation. All 99 upgrade tests
pass. No new drift was found on re-verification. This QA cycle is complete.
